/**

@page page_exahype_domain-decomposition Domain decomposition

\tableofcontents

ExaHyPE uses Peano's deterministic rank ownership and the dispatches over the
rank-local mesh. @ref page_peano_domain_decomposition describes the tree, the
mesh, the halo exchange and the migration. @ref page_peano_runtime_analysis
describes the reported balance metrics.

# Solver data

Each MPI rank owns its solver repository and the rank-local cell and face
pools of every solver. Cells belong to one rank. A face on a rank boundary
exists in the partition of both ranks; a solver writes the half of its local
cell and receives the other half through peano4::HaloExchange before the face
dispatch reads it.

# Solver state

A solver keeps one time stamp and one time step size per rank. A dispatch
reduces its per-cell results, such as the maximum wave speed, through
tarch::accelerator::parallelReduce, and the solver reduces the result across
the ranks with one collective at the end of the time step. The tarch MPI
wrappers provide these reductions with the runtime's timeout and progress
handling.

ExaHyPE applies this pattern to the admissible time step size, the mesh width
bounds, the troubled-cell count of the limiter and the error measurements.

# Adaptive ownership

ExaHyPE commits cell-based refine and erase requests through
exahype2::RefinementRequests. Every rank derives the same target tree and the
same cell-weighted rank ownership from that set, and every solver's
`regrid()` moves its cell pools through the peano4::Regrid.

The geometric partition uses cell counts. Particle-heavy cells, limited cells
or heterogeneous kernels can therefore produce application load imbalance on
a geometrically balanced grid.

*/
